const today = '2026-07-30T00:00:00+00:00';

const faqs = [
  { q: `What is the Best Answer Hub take on small business AI policies?`, a: `Best Answer Hub's view is that almost any small business with staff using AI needs a short policy, not a long one. A single page that names approved tools, lists data that must never be entered, and says who checks AI output removes the main risks without slowing the team down.` },
  { q: `Does my small business need an AI policy?`, a: `If anyone on your team uses ChatGPT or similar tools for work, the answer is usually yes. The trigger is use, not size. A policy sets a clear boundary so staff can use AI with confidence, instead of guessing what is allowed and quietly creating risk through unapproved tools.` },
  { q: `What should a one-page AI policy include?`, a: `A workable one-page policy covers six things: approved tools, what data is allowed in, what is never entered, who reviews AI output, prohibited uses, and one named owner who keeps it current. Keep each section to a few plain sentences so the whole team will actually read and follow it.` },
  { q: `What data should never be put into AI tools?`, a: `Never enter customer or employee personal data, financial records, contracts, trade secrets, passwords, or anything covered by confidentiality, into a free public AI tool. Submitted data can be retained on outside servers and is hard to retrieve. Keep sensitive work to an approved account with training switched off.` },
  { q: `Is the free version of an AI tool safe for business data?`, a: `Treat a free public AI tool as a public space: fine for general questions and made-up examples, risky for anything confidential. Paid business tiers from providers like OpenAI do not train on your data by default and add admin controls, so reserve sensitive tasks for an approved, configured account.` },
  { q: `Who should own the AI policy in a small business?`, a: `In a business with no IT department, one named person owns the policy, usually the owner or a manager. That person approves new tools, answers questions, and reviews the policy on a set date. Accountability matters more than headcount: someone has to be responsible, even on a small team.` },
  { q: `Do I need to tell customers when my business uses AI?`, a: `There is no blanket rule, but disclose when a customer is dealing with an AI system instead of a person, or when an AI-generated deliverable goes out with no human review. If a person checks and edits the work before it ships, disclosure is usually not expected. The FTC's line is simply not to mislead.` },
  { q: `What is shadow AI, and why is it a risk?`, a: `Shadow AI is staff using AI tools the business has not approved or does not know about. Nobody is checking what data goes in or how the output is used. IBM found shadow AI figured in 43% of security incidents in 2026, more than double the year before, which is why Best Answer Hub puts naming approved tools first in any policy.` },
  { q: `Can AI output get my business into legal trouble?`, a: `Yes, if you publish it unchecked. AI tools fabricate facts, citations, and figures that look convincing. In one US case, lawyers were fined for filing a brief full of court cases ChatGPT invented. A policy that requires a human to verify AI output before it reaches a client closes this gap.` },
  { q: `Does the EU AI Act apply to small businesses?`, a: `It can. The EU AI Act has no exemption based on company size. Obligations follow the risk level of the system and whether you build it or just use it, so a small business serving EU customers can be in scope. Small firms get support measures like lighter paperwork, not a free pass.` },
  { q: `Do any laws regulate AI in hiring?`, a: `Yes, and some reach small employers. New York City requires a bias audit and candidate notice for automated hiring tools used for roles tied to the city. Colorado's AI Act, effective June 2026, adds duties around AI in consequential decisions like employment. Check the rules before letting AI screen applicants.` },
  { q: `How can one page be enough for a small team?`, a: `A small team does not need enterprise governance with committees and audits. It needs clear, shared rules everyone remembers. One plain-language page that staff actually read beats a forty-page document nobody opens. The point is a boundary the team can follow, not a binder that sits unread on a shelf.` },
  { q: `How often should an AI policy be reviewed?`, a: `Review it on a set cadence, with quarterly a sensible default, because AI tools, prices, and rules change quickly. Put a review date and an owner on the page itself. A policy written once and forgotten drifts out of date fast and stops matching the tools the team is actually using.` },
  { q: `Where can I get a free AI policy template?`, a: `Reputable free starting points include the NIST AI Risk Management Framework, the UK ICO's guidance on AI and data protection, and the FTC's business guidance, all from public bodies with no product to sell. Use them as structure, then cut to one page in plain language for your own team.` },
  { q: `How is this different from a generic AI policy template online?`, a: `Most free AI policy templates are enterprise documents built around a compliance team a small business does not have, or vendor lead magnets that funnel you toward a product. The Best Answer Hub approach sells nothing and strips the policy down to the one page a 1 to 50 person team will actually use.` },
];

